/*
  =====================================================================
  HOW KEYS RESOLVE

  1. TOGGLE rows declare no strings in BHTSettings.m. The UI derives them
     from the toggle's own key:
         some_toggle_key -> "SOME_TOGGLE_KEY_TITLE"   (row title)
                         -> "SOME_TOGGLE_KEY_DETAIL"  (grey subtitle)
     _DETAIL is optional: omit it and no subtitle is drawn.
  2. BUTTON rows declare "titleKey" / "subtitleKey" explicitly.
     compactButton rows instead show a live value via "prefKeyForSubtitle",
     so they intentionally have no _DETAIL key.
  3. GROUP HEADERS use "<PAGE>_GROUP_<NAME>". Sentence case, like the row
     titles: the header cell prints them exactly as written, so the case you
     type is the case on screen.
     A page earns headers from roughly ten rows up. Below that it stays flat —
     two headers over four rows is decoration, not structure.
  4. PAGES use "MODERN_SETTINGS_<PAGE>_TITLE" / "_SUBTITLE".
     Note: the General page's keys are historically named _LAYOUT_.

  A missing key renders as the raw key name on screen.

  WRITING RULES (keep new strings consistent)

  TITLES — sentence case, no final period, 4 words max.
  Rows that REMOVE, REVEAL or SWITCH OFF something use a closed family:
      Hide <thing>      removes UI            "Hide bookmarks"
      Show <thing>      adds UI               "Show Tweet source labels"
      Restore <thing>   brings back classic   "Restore reply context"
      Disable <thing>   turns a behaviour off "Disable auto-translate"
  Rows that PERFORM an action use that action's own verb: Confirm, Save,
  Download, Copy, Lock, Clean, Bypass, Preload.
  Rows that CHANGE a property are bare noun phrases: "Square profile
  pictures", "Tab labels", "Scroll bar".
  Buttons opening another screen are always bare nouns: "Theme color".
  Never "No ...", "Turn off ...", "Use ...", "Always ...".

  SUBTITLES (row) — exactly one sentence, ending with a period, describing
  what happens when the row is ON. Max ~80 characters. Never restate the
  title. Prefer the dominant openers: "Remove ..." and "Show ...".

  BODY TEXT (screen-level explanations such as the theme picker intro or the
  navigation bar editor hint) may run to two sentences — they are
  instructions, not row subtitles.

  Sections below mirror the settings menu in display order; pickers, editors
  and alerts follow the page that owns them.
  =====================================================================
*/

